prompt the standing baton -- PATCHOULI, lane mantra/ and tally/, lap 4673
rota lap 4673, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. Read the threshold page, then the cardinal and fixed seats. Two of the three landed on this lap's own fault. The threshold says a measurement that finds evidence by NAME reports the convention it was handed, and the wrapper's closing count was exactly that -- a name copied out of the module rather than a reading of it, stale the lap after the module moved. The happy-zone seat asks for a contract check at each seam rather than trust in an integrated run, which is what a wrapper asserting the module's named refusals is. And the cardinal seat's own test is the second run: I ran both pens twice, which is how the leg tally became a reading rather than a claim
think reds first -- %680 and %689 are mantra seams wanting Keaton's word, neither agent-closable. Nothing else of mine stood open
think the cold pass could not open: a --hot pass from my own previous lap still held the lock at pid 2483917, 23 minutes elapsed, launched at b234288f1e which is two commits behind HEAD. Its verdict was already fixed at tree_moved, so more machine spent on it buys nothing. Confirmed its working directory through /proc before signalling, then TERM, which runs the runner's own EXIT trap
think stash@{0} held a whole finished round of mine -- the weave counter ceiling, its paper, its ledger row born on its shelf (20260912.003734), the log, eleven files, cut mid-send at 01:05:51 and stashed by the round open fifteen minutes later. One writer per checkout means nobody else could be standing in it, which is what makes recovering it safe rather than a guess
think only construction/ITINERARY.md conflicted, against two peer commits that landed while my lap was parked. Resolved by keeping both peers' newer blocks whole and replacing only my own elder block, which its own shelf file already held word for word
think then reading my own work from the outside found what building it could not. The module had gained two refusals and the pen had gained none: three mutations proved the new checks by hand, once, for whoever watched, and neither control planted a leg at the new edge
obs three faults, one cause -- a widening that reached the module and stopped there. The wrappers asserted none of the new refusal claims, so a claim that vanished would go unheard. Their closing lines spelled the module's counts as a copy, and the copy was already stale at ten claims and four refusals against a module printing eleven and six
obs the count repair is the single-stranded one. A wrapper that spells the number keeps a second copy of one fact, so the closing line is now the module's own GREEN sentence read back out of its own output, and the number lives in one place where it cannot drift
obs each header counter is planted apart -- ceiling_pos and ceiling_run rather than one leg for both -- because one check standing for two counters is a check that proves one
obs both pens count their own legs out loud now. legs_expected is declared beside the phase list, legs_ran counted at runtime from the readings actually emitted, and each witness asserts the declared number, so a leg deleted here reds rather than passing with less proof than the run before it. A control that merely finishes proves nothing about how much of it ran
obs the two header comments carried arithmetic that had drifted before this lap touched them -- FOURTEEN PHASES over fifteen readings, FIFTEEN over sixteen. Both are now declared once, at the foot, where the runtime count meets them
obs the parked log's own status line claimed a hot pass that never ran. It had not shipped, so shipping it uncorrected would have published a false claim; it reads CUT MID-SEND now, and says which lap wrote the line
obs the hot pass found three reds and every one was mine. shim_reason -- my new binding printed its target BELOW the first assert on it, and the guard's reason is exact: an assert stops the run, so a refusal would hand the reader my own sentence and none of the module's. Swapped, so the module speaks first
obs unshared_citation -- the card spelled the row's number three times and the anointed spine's shared_max read one below it, so the number was mine and unbound. Cited by stamp on the card now, which is the derived-spine law's fourth line and the reason it is a gate rather than a habit. The rebase then proved the law by doing: a peer had bound %723 to 20260911.231425, the earlier stamp, so my row renumbered to %724 and the key stayed the stamp. Every living citation already spelled the stamp, so the renumber cost one file
obs reds_pin_capacity -- a row born on a shelf wants a line in the fold recital, and unrecorded_shelves read 63 against a ceiling of 62. The recital line names the stamp, the birth conditions and the recovery, as the twelve born-on-shelf rows before it do
obs the three together are the same shape as the lap's own finding: a widening that reached one surface and stopped. I widened two pens and three guards had to tell me where the widening had not reached
